From 8ad5282bb2bb91330bdb0708237e9caa6dbe9d1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:33:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] fix(engine): follow TLC where a lazy set with a part it
 can't size differs from its enumeration

TLC keeps S \X T, [a : S, b : T] and [S -> T] lazy (SetOfTuplesValue,
SetOfRcdsValue, SetOfFcnsValue), and with them a \cup, \cap or \ it
doesn't reduce. It compares, fingerprints, sizes and enumerates such a
set as its enumeration, which decides emptiness before it asks a part,
so the set enumeration the engine holds, Int \X {} as {}, answers alike
there, and the trace evaluator, which prints a trace's states and
explains a failure, prints the set as TLC does. Where TLC differs, the
engine now follows it:

- TLC reduces an intersection or a difference with such a set by
  testing the other operand's elements in it (Reducible.cap), which
  fails on a value of the wrong kind, so (Int \X {}) \cap {"a"} fails
  as in TLC rather than being {}. An unreduced \cap or \ of lazy sets is
  irreducible, as SetCapValue and SetDiffValue are, and a quantifier's
  bound, a set map, = and the left of \subseteq enumerate the set where
  TLC does.
- IsFiniteSet of one is TLC's isFinite of the set as TLC holds it.
- TLC prints such a set by its parts where it can't size one before an
  empty one (SetOfRcdsValue.toString), and fails so in PrintT and
  ToString. The engine records that the run enumerated such a set
  (Program::holds_collapsed), and a write of a value that may hold one
  declines: Print, PrintT, AssertEq and ToString, -dump, a simulation's
  trace, and a constant conjunct's message.
- A function over a \cup, \cap or \ that TLC enumerates is built, as
  TLC's toFcnRcd builds it to print or compare it.

The startup fixtures lazy-* pin each case against TLC, and
native/design/fallback.md lists the writes that decline and the
intersections TLC doesn't reduce that the engine still answers
otherwise, as on main.
